sections.content:
The two-axis model (the key mental shift), verified conceptually:

```css
/* The container — one axis is main, the other is cross */
.navbar {
  display: flex;            /* children become flex items */
  flex-direction: row;       /* main axis: horizontal (default) */
  justify-content: space-between;  /* main-axis: logo left, links right */
  align-items: center;       /* cross-axis: vertically centered */
  gap: 16px;                 /* spacing between items — no margin hacks */
}

/* The children — the flex trio */
.sidebar { flex: 0 0 260px; }   /* fixed: don't grow, don't shrink, 260px */
.content { flex: 1; }            /* 1 1 0: absorbs ALL remaining space */

/* The famous centering — three declarations */
.center-everything {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;   /* main axis */
  align-items: center;        /* cross axis */
}
```

The flex shorthand decoded (the interview core):
- flex: 1 → 1 1 0%: grow 1 share, shrinkable, basis 0 → equal-width columns regardless of content
- flex: auto → 1 1 auto: grows AND sizes from its content first
- flex: none → 0 0 auto: refuses to grow or shrink (fixed)
- flex-grow 2 vs 1: two items with grow 2 and 1 split free space 2:1 — grow is a RATIO, not a size

When Flexbox vs Grid (the decision question): Flexbox = one axis, content-driven, 'distribute these along a line' (it wraps but each line is independent). Grid = two axes, container-driven, 'place these in a coordinate system.' The navbar/toolbar/split-pane is flex; the page layout/dashboard/magazine-grid is Grid. They compose: a Grid page area containing a flex toolbar.
